  "query_preview": "/Through Friday/...\nIssued at 111 PM MDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nOur main concern late this afternoon and evening will be another\nround of strong thunderstorms, capable of producing flash flooding\nand severe weather. Nearly full sunshine over the plains has\nallowed the airmass to destabilize through early this afternoon. \nLow level moisture was rather impressive with dewpoints holding in\nthe lower to mid 60s east of the I-25 Corridor. That's pushing \nMLCAPE values into the 1000-2000 J/kg range per latest SPC \nanalysis. While bulk shear is marginal (only around 25 kts) for \norganized severe storms, we have sufficient instability to drive \nlarge hail and damaging wet microbursts from stronger storms. \nWhile there was some drying noted in the 700-600 mb layer on the \nmost recent ACARS soundings, we still expect efficient rain \nproducers given low level moisture and updraft intensity, and \nenough to warrant a Flood Watch for flash flooding in areas that \nsaw recent heavy rains - see HYDROLOGY section below for more \ndetails. \n\nLike most convective episodes, there will probably be some weaker\nconvection that lingers behind the initial stronger storms that\npush east across the plains. Otherwise look for gradual clearing \novernight, but patchy fog/stratus development once again with a \nvery moist boundary layer. \n\nOn Saturday, deep layer shear increases as flow aloft strengthens\nfrom the west-southwest. At the same time, we'll still have a fair\namount of low/mid level moisture present, which means growing \ninstability and another round of afternoon and evening storms. \nSome of these may be severe again due to increased shear but \na little decrease in instability as some slight drying is still \nforecast. \n\nSunday is shaping up to be another active weather day, and\npotentially even higher than Saturday as persistent low level\neasterly flow and moisture advection occurs over the plains. A\nfailure mode would be too much moisture/low level cloudiness over\nthe plains, but at the same time that could mean even greater\ninstability and more favorable shear profiles if we get enough\nheating to break the cap. \n\nWe're still on course for drier weather Monday and Tuesday as the\nridge axis retrogrades slightly. That allows for a drier west-\nnorthwest flow aloft and warm advection. High temperatures will\nlikely (>70% chance) return to 90F+ by Tuesday. \n\nWednesday should feature higher chances of storms again (30-50%\nchance), as another front is scheduled to bring slight cooling \nbut moistening late Tuesday into Wednesday. We don't see much \nchange in the overall pattern through the end of next week, which \nat this point favors fairly normal Colorado weather with near \nnormal temperatures and isolated to scattered afternoon and \nevening storms each day.",
